der-pay-gap-is-not-what-activists-claim</a><br /> Equal pay statistics are bogus because they don\u2019t compare like with like<br /> <a href=\"http://www.telegraph.co.uk/comment/columnists/vickiwoods/7957186/Sorry-ladies-Im-not-worried-about-wage-gaps.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.telegraph.co.uk/comment/c...wage-gaps.html</a><br /> Fair Pay Isn\u2019t Always Equal Pay<br /> <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2010/09/22/opinion/22Sommers.html?_r=1&amp;hp\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.nytimes.com/2010/09/22/op...s.html?_r=1&amp;hp</a><br /> The Wage Gap Myth<br /> <a href=\"http://www.americanthinker.com/2010/09/the_wage_gap_myth.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.americanthinker.com/2010/..._gap_myth.html</a><br /> Don\u2019t Blame Discrimination for Gender Wage Gap<br /> <a href=\"http://www.bloomberg.com/news/2012-08-13/don-t-blame-discrimination-for-gender-wage-gap.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.bloomberg.com/news/2012-0...-wage-gap.html</a><br /> The pay inequality myth: Women are more equal than you think<br /> <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qa3pKN3XUKM&amp;feature=youtu.be\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qa3pK...ature=youtu.be</a><br /> Women Now a Majority in American Workplaces<br /> <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2010/02/06/business/economy/06women.html?_r=2\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.nytimes.com/2010/02/06/bu...omen.html?_r=2</a><br /> Labor force participation rate for men has never been lower.<br /> <a href=\"http://www.zerohedge.com/news/biggest-shock-fridays-payroll-report-sorry-men\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.zerohedge.com/news/bigges...port-sorry-men</a><br /> Share of Men in Labor Force at All-Time Low<br /> <a href=\"http://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/share-of-men-in-labor-force-at-all-time-low/?src=recg\" target=\"_blank\">http://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/20...-low/?src=recg</a><br /> Women In Tech Make More Money And Land Better Jobs Than Men<br /> <a href=\"http://www.businessinsider.com/women-in-tech-make-more-money-and-land-better-jobs-than-men-2010-9\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.businessinsider.com/women...han-men-2010-9</a><br /> Female U.S. corporate directors out-earn men: study<br /> <a href=\"http://www.reuters.com/article/domesticNews/idUSN0752118220071107?feedType=R\" target=\"_blank\">www.reuters.com/article/domesticNews/idUSN0752118220071107?feedType=R</a><br /> Female CEOs outearned men in 2009.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://abcnews.go.com/print?id=10630664\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://s.abcnews.com/images/site/printlogo.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://abcnews.go.com/print?id=10630664\" target=\"_blank\"> Women CEOs Beat Men in Pay in 2009 </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://abcnews.go.com/print?id=10630664\" target=\"_blank\"> http://abcnews.go.com/print?id=10630664 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> Women between ages 21 and 30 working full-time made 117% of men\u2019s wages.<br /> <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2007/08/03/nyregion/03women.html?_r=1&amp;oref=slogin\" target=\"_blank\">www.nytimes.com/2007/08/03/nyregion/03women.html?_r=1&amp;oref=slogin</a><br /> According to the U.S. Census Bureau, single women between 22 and 30 years old earn an average of $27,000 a year. That\u2019s 8% more than comparable men.<br /> <a href=\"http://www.ksee24.com/news/local/Young-Women-Earn-More-159818705.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.ksee24.com/news/local/You...159818705.html</a><br /> Workplace Salaries: At Last, Women on Top<br /> <a href=\"http://www.time.com/time/business/article/0,8599,2015274,00.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.time.com/time/business/ar...015274,00.html</a><br /> Young Women\u2019s Pay Exceeds Male Peers<br /> <a href=\"http://online.wsj.com/article/SB10001424052748704421104575463790770831192.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://online.wsj.com/article/SB1000...770831192.html</a><br /> The 15 Jobs Where Women Earn More Than Men<br /> <a href=\"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jennagoudreau/2011/03/14/jobs-where-women-earn-more-than-men/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.forbes.com/sites/jennagou...more-than-men/</a><br /> Women aged between 22 and 29 earn over \u00a310 per hour on average, compared to men their same age who earn just under this amount.<br /> <a href=\"http://www.womenintechnology.co.uk/news/young-women-earn-more-than-men%E2%80%94news-800761492\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.womenintechnology.co.uk/news/young-women-earn-more-than-men\u2014news-800761492</a><br /> Young women now earn more than men in UK <a href=\"http://www.womensviewsonnews.org/2011/10/young-women-now-earn-more-than-men-in-uk/\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.womensviewsonnews.org/201...han-men-in-uk/</a><br /> The only chairwoman in the FTSE 100 index of biggest British companies, when asked about government efforts to force companies to make at least 25% of board member to be female said: \u201cthere\u2019s no real evidence to suggest women being on a board makes the companies any better \u2013 what we\u2019re doing here is forcing an experiment.\u201d<br /> This was further supported in the book \u201cWhy Men Earn More\u201d by Warren Farrell, Ph.D., examined 25 career/life choices men and women make (hours, commute times, etc.) that lead to men earning more and women having more balanced lives, and that showed how men in surveys prioritize money while women prioritize flexibility, shorter hours, shorter commutes, less physical risk and other factors conducive to their choice to be primary parents, an option men still largely don\u2019t have. That is why never-married childless women outearn their male counterparts, and female corporate directors now outearn their male counterparts.<br /> <a href=\"http://www.reuters.com/article/domesticNews/idUSN0752118220071107?feedTy%E2%80%A6\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.reuters.com/article/domesticNews/idUSN0752118220071107?feedTy\u2026</a><br /> Farrell also lists dozens of careers, including fields of science, where women outearn men. Women simply have more options than men to be primary parents, and many of them exercise that option rather than work long, stressful hours. That is why 57% of female graduates of Stanford and Harvard left the workforce within 15 years of entry into the workforce.<br /> <a href=\"http://edition.cnn.com/2005/BUSINESS/03/15/optout.revolution/\" target=\"_blank\">http://edition.cnn.com/2005/BUSINESS...ut.revolution/</a><br /> This is an option few men have (try being a single male and telling women on the first date that you want to stay home).<br /> Blaming men for women\u2019s choices is unfair. In fact research shows most men have no problem with their wives outearning them.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/23413243\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/23413243\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/23413243\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/23413243 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> Research also shows most working dads would quit or take a pay cut to spend more time with kids if their spouses could support the family.<br /> <a href=\"http://www.cnn.com/2007/US/Careers/06/13/dads.work/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.cnn.com/2007/US/Careers/0...ork/index.html</a><br /> Research also shows that parents share workloads more when mothers allow men to be primary parents.<br /> <a href=\"http://www.usatoday.com/news/health/2009-05-04-equal-parenting_N.htm\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.usatoday.com/news/health/...arenting_N.htm</a><br /> ABC News: \u201cIs the Wage Gap Women\u2019s Choice? Research Suggests Career Decisions, Not Sex Bias, Are at Root of Pay Disparity\u201d<br /> <a href=\"http://abcnews.go.com/2020/GiveMeABreak/story?id=797045&amp;page=1&amp;CMP=OTC-R\" target=\"_blank\">http://abcnews.go.com/2020/GiveMeABr...ge=1&amp;CMP=OTC-R</a><br /> There is also the myth that women are kept out of certain more lucrative fields by sexism. The truth is that women stay away from math out of their own free choice<br /> <a href=\"http://sify.com/news/women-stay-away-from-math-out-of-their-own-free-choice-news-scitech-kk1lubiiiee.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://sify.com/news/women-stay-away...1lubiiiee.html</a><br /> Women In Science: No Discrimination, Says Cornell Study<br /> <a href=\"http://www.science20.com/news_articles/women_science_no_discrimination_says_cornell_study-75984\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.science20.com/news_articl...ll_study-75984</a><br /> Let\u2019s be real about the lack of women in tech<br /> <a href=\"http://www.businessinsider.com/lets-be-real-about-the-lack-of-women-in-tech-2010-10\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.businessinsider.com/lets-...n-tech-2010-10</a><br /> Here are some by women<br /> <a href=\"http://www.payscale.com/career-news/2009/12/do-men-or-women-choose-majors-to-maximize-income\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.payscale.com/career-news/...aximize-income</a><br /> <a href=\"http://online.wsj.com/article/SB10001424052748704415104576250672504707048.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://online.wsj.com/article/SB1000...504707048.html</a><br /> US Department of Labor<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://social.dol.gov/blog/myth-busting-the-pay-gap/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://social.dol.gov/blog/myth-busting-the-pay-gap/\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://social.dol.gov/blog/myth-busting-the-pay-gap/\" target=\"_blank\"> http://social.dol.gov/blog/myth-busting-the-pay-gap/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> Rape culture? A myth. The Rape, Abuse &amp; Incest National Network has done extensive research and has dismissed it. <a href=\"http://rainn.org/images/03-2014/WH-Task-Force-RAINN-Recommendations.pdf\" target=\"_blank\"><b>RAINN has officially dismantled feminist rape culture hysteria.</b></a><a href=\"http://www.usnews.com/opinion/blogs/economic-intelligence/2013/10/24/statistics-dont-back-up-claims-about-rape-culture\" target=\"_blank\">Statistics don\u2019t back up Rape culture</a><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FKgrYVtYSCk\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FKgrYVtYSCk\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FKgrYVtYSCk\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FKgrYVtYSCk </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lNsJ1DhqQ-s\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lNsJ1DhqQ-s\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lNsJ1DhqQ-s\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lNsJ1DhqQ-s </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> Domestic violence? There\u2019s no patriarchy involved there.<ul><li><a href=\"http://pubpages.unh.edu/%7Emas2/V75-Straus-09.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">Women are perpetrators often as men.</a></li> <li><a href=\"http://www.csulb.edu/%7Emfiebert/assault.htm\" target=\"_blank\">Domestic violence being equally committed by women, only males get arrested</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://www.uncfsp.org/projects/userfiles/File/DCE-STOP_NOW/NCADV_LGBT_Fact_Sheet.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">Gay and bisexual men experience abuse in intimate partner relationships at a rate of 2 in 5, which is comparable to the amount of domestic violence experienced by heterosexual women. </a></li> <li><a href=\"https://www.musc.edu/vawprevention/lesbianrx/factsheet.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">About 17-45% of lesbians report having been the victim of a least one act of physical violence perpetrated by a lesbian partner (1,5,6,13).</a></li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>And so on and so forth. Long story short, the issue is marginally more complex then it is generally portrayed. Feminism is a good thing, but much of the movement has been turned towards...well, awful ends or at least ones that aren't supported by...well, reality.<br /> <br /> Sorry if the formats a bit screwy; copy/paste and all that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1020,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1020-ryuugi","user":"Ryuugi","date":". 10-31-2014, 01:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1020-ryuugi","name":"Ryuugi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1020_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-30T23:16:43-07:00","id":297854,"title":"","content":"Oh my god.<br /> <br /> Edit: I debated for a while whether I should reply or not, and I won't go into details except in one case. I will admit that some of those points are true. There are stereotypes that harm men, just like those that harm women. There are some situations in which women are more privileged. And, big surprise, sometimes feminist do some dumb shit.<br /> <br /> I take issue with a few things. For one, the definite article before, and the singular form on, the word &quot;movement&quot;. Feminism isn't one movement. There are several, sometimes complementary, sometimes contradictory, sometimes outright at war with each other. Just like in politics, everyone doesn't agree with each other, and the most current trend in the field is to diversify and open for <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intersectionality\" target=\"_blank\">intersectional</a> analyses where previously there were. The fact that one disagrees with some feminist statements isn't a reason to declare the &quot;movement&quot; off-track. That's like saying that since one doesn't like this one political party, democracy is dead.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, without going postmodernist on you here, I'll ask you to take care when you talk about &quot;reality&quot;. Turns out, reality can be quite different for different people. Again, see intersectionality. It's important to stay humble in the face of situations one is less familiar with, less one believes one's frame of reference always applies.<br /> <br /> Connecting back to the above, the fact that there are more studies needed from the academic field of men's studies does not mean that feminism has gone too far or is off-track, either. It just means that men's studies have not done enough yet. <hr /><br /> And, the one thing I will comment on from your list there: the point saying that &quot;feminist rape culture hysteria&quot; is &quot;dismantled&quot;. Have you read the article? It says nothing of the sort. First of all, both articles are looking at university campus areas only and so can only draw conclusions about those. It might easily exist on other geographical areas, or in other segments of society that don't usually go to college, despite not being obvious there.<br /> <br /> And in neither case do they draw the conclusion that rape culture is disproven even on those premises. The first one says that we should blame the rapists, not the rape culture, and I <i>totally agree</i> with that. In no way should we let rapists use rape culture as some sort of mitigating circumstance; that's like saying I couldn't be expected to drive the car properly since I was dead drunk at the time. It argues that most rape and sexual assaults are committed by repeat offenders, and I believe that, too.<br /> <br /> The second one argues that there is not a roaring rape rampage going on at campuses and that a false rape accusation is also a horrible thing. I agree, it is, and in my mind should be fully as punishable as proper rape. In that article, though, I feel it gets applied disingenuously, like someone felt they had to reply just because. I guess I agree with some of the points made, but not with all (without more evidence) and certainly not the tone.<br /> <br /> Both articles count incidents of rape and sexual assault as indicative of a non-presence of rape culture. However, if you stroll on over to Wikipedia (and if you don't trust them, there are hand references at the bottom), you'll find that <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rape_culture\" target=\"_blank\">rape culture is about so much more than the number of rapes in an area</a>. Counting numbers isn't going to address the issue, even if, y'know, less rape is good, of course.<br /> <br /> So no. Looking at the signs of rape culture, and looking at the western world today, rape culture is still truckin'.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Dex Davican wrote:</b> I can say without exaggeration or dishonesty that I am the most creative man <i>ever 